00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.770
on a secondary provider,
then C-SEK is the way to go.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.550
So I'll be delving into
what each of these offer.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:41.787
So first, default
Google encryption.

00:05:41.787 --> 00:05:43.620
How does Google encrypt
everything at scale?

00:05:43.620 --> 00:05:45.139
Because it's massive.

00:05:45.139 --> 00:05:47.430
So to understand how we do
that, you have to understand

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.380
how we store data at Google.

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:51.390
When an object is
uploaded, for example

00:05:51.390 --> 00:05:53.310
to Google Cloud
Storage, we shard

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.740
or chunk that object
into smaller pieces.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:59.070
And then each of those chunks
gets a locally-generated data

00:05:59.070 --> 00:06:01.520
encryption key, or a DEK.

00:06:01.520 --> 00:06:04.360
That key is generated on that
machine that writes that thing.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:07.170
I'm talking about storage
layer encryption here.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:10.800
Each chunk will have a
unique data encryption key,

00:06:10.800 --> 00:06:13.080
even if it's part of the
same Google Cloud Storage

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.517
object as another chunk,
even if it belongs

00:06:15.517 --> 00:06:17.100
to the same customer
as another chunk,

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:19.680
even if it's sitting on the
same machine as another chunk.

00:06:19.680 --> 00:06:22.350
We have a super small,
low-level isolation--

00:06:22.350 --> 00:06:25.260
cryptographical isolation--

00:06:25.260 --> 00:06:26.370
for how data is stored.

00:06:28.981 --> 00:06:30.480
Access to those
chunks is controlled

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:34.410
through access control
lists at a per-chunk level.

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:37.230
So the storage
system controls who

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.440
can actually access those chunks
and those keys at that level.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.350
Those chunks are distributed
across Google's infrastructure

00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:45.690
for latency, backup,
storage, et cetera.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.524
And we also actually
backup data encrypted.

00:06:47.524 --> 00:06:49.065
We don't decrypt it
and re-encrypt it

00:06:49.065 --> 00:06:49.800
in the new backup system.

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.591
We just take it and
move it somewhere else,

00:06:51.591 --> 00:06:54.090
and it's backed up that way.

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:59.340
So if someone were to try to
walk into Google and steal--

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:03.840
steal hardware, steal something
physical-- and take your data,

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:07.430
they would first have to
know where all the chunks are

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:09.540
that their object
has been split into,

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:13.020
which is a really non-trivial
problem at Google's scale,

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:15.780
have access to all those chunks,
and have access to all the keys

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.910
that encrypt those chunks.

00:07:17.910 --> 00:07:19.380
So where are those keys stored?

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:20.880
Well actually, you
can think of them

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:23.910
as being stored as, like, a
header to that data chunk.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:26.250
But of course, we're not
going to write data encryption

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:27.480
keys in plain text--

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:28.677
no, no, no.

00:07:28.677 --> 00:07:30.760
We're going to encrypt
those data encryption keys.

00:07:30.760 --> 00:07:32.850
So we encrypt them with another
key called the key encryption

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:33.350
key.

00:07:33.350 --> 00:07:35.650
This is the hierarchy that
I was mentioning earlier.

00:07:35.650 --> 00:07:38.314
So we have data encrypted
by data encryption keys,

00:07:38.314 --> 00:07:40.480
and data encryption keys
encrypted by key encryption

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:41.710
keys.

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.730
Those key encryption keys sit
inside Google's internal Key

00:07:44.730 --> 00:07:48.840
Management Service, or KMS, and
KMS was built for that purpose.

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:52.200
So KMS does not allow
keys to be exported.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.405
Rather, the data
encryption keys go

00:07:54.405 --> 00:07:57.710
to KMS to be encrypted and
re-encrypted, and sent back.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.190
That allows us to
have auditability

00:07:59.190 --> 00:08:01.440
over how those key
encryption keys are used.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:03.600
That also allows us to
have those tight ACLs

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:08.260
that we want, again, on how the
usage of those key encryption

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:12.900
keys by services
and users is done.

00:08:12.900 --> 00:08:15.890
KMS is highly scalable,
and it has to be,

00:08:15.890 --> 00:08:17.670
again, to operate
things at Google scale.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:21.660
So it's incredibly low latency,
high availability, and globally

00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:22.260
available.

00:08:22.260 --> 00:08:24.009
And what that actually
means, in practice,

00:08:24.009 --> 00:08:26.070
is that our KMS runs
on tens of thousands

00:08:26.070 --> 00:08:29.220
of production machines in
Google data centers globally.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.830
So when you actually are
pinging something and getting

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:34.000
a piece of data back, there
is an encryption process

00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:34.500
happening.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.780
And it's hitting a
local instance of KMS

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.450
in order to decrypt that
data and get it back to you.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:43.270
That's what allows us to have
encryption at scale at Google.

00:08:43.270 --> 00:08:45.600
All right, so you guys
are like, OK, I got--

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.130
you know, I'm skimming a slide.

00:08:47.130 --> 00:08:50.070
So first, how does
that work in practice?

00:08:50.070 --> 00:08:51.570
So you can think
about this as being

00:08:51.570 --> 00:08:53.530
three different actors here.

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:56.360
There's a service
that's using the data.

00:08:56.360 --> 00:08:59.400
There is a storage system that's
storing the encrypted data.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:02.080
And there's a KMS that's
storing the encryption keys--

00:09:02.080 --> 00:09:03.530
the key encryption keys.

00:09:03.530 --> 00:09:05.280
So what happens in
practice is the service

00:09:05.280 --> 00:09:08.680
asks the storage
system for some object.

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:11.940
The storage system verifies
that the service has the right

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:16.830
to access that object,
figures out all the chunks

00:09:16.830 --> 00:09:19.790
in which that data is
stored, checks those ACLs.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:22.350
Then it pulls the
data encryption keys

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:24.450
that are sitting with
those chunks of data,

00:09:24.450 --> 00:09:28.950
and passes those data encryption
keys, encrypted, to the KMS.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.170
The KMS then verifies
that the storage system--

00:09:31.170 --> 00:09:32.670
again, another ACL
check-- that it

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:36.030
has the right to access
those key encryption keys,

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.580
decrypts those data
encryption keys in memory,

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.130
sends them back to
the storage system.

00:09:41.130 --> 00:09:42.751
And people often ask me--

00:09:42.751 --> 00:09:45.000
that connection here between
the storages and the KMS,

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:46.470
that's an encrypted RPC.

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:49.949
So you're not sending around
plaintext keys on our network.

00:09:49.949 --> 00:09:51.990
Then from the storage
system back to the service,

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:53.820
the storage system
decrypts the data,

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.340
and sends back the plaintext
data to the service,

00:09:56.340 --> 00:09:56.970
in most cases.

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.507
In some cases, the service
decrypts it directly.

00:09:59.507 --> 00:10:00.840
Again, that's a general example.

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:06.930
For something like SSDs, it's
actually a locally-managed key

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.190
for the SSD at the DEK level.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:12.882
But this, in general, is how
storage works in our systems--

00:10:12.882 --> 00:10:15.600
sorry, encryption and
storage work in our systems.

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:17.610
So you're like, OK, we
got data, data encryption

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:20.026
keys, key encryption keys--
it's turtles all the way down,

00:10:20.026 --> 00:10:20.910
right?

00:10:20.910 --> 00:10:22.860
Kind of.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:25.860
So what happens next?

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:27.340
This is the fun part.

00:10:27.340 --> 00:10:30.830
So we said earlier, we have
some blue-- data, the yellow--

00:10:30.830 --> 00:10:33.390
data encryption key,
green-- key encryption key.

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.860
And that key encryption key
is stored inside our KMS.

00:10:36.860 --> 00:10:39.380
All right, but we're not going
to write keys to disk, right?

00:10:39.380 --> 00:10:40.140
We said that earlier.

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:42.306
That's not going to happen,
plain text keys to disk.

00:10:42.306 --> 00:10:44.484
So when KMS is not
running in memory,

00:10:44.484 --> 00:10:45.650
where are those keys stored?

00:10:45.650 --> 00:10:49.520
Well of course, they're
stored encrypted on disk.

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.790
So they are wrapped
with a KMS master key.

00:10:52.790 --> 00:10:55.730
And that KMS master
key sits in another KMS

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:57.650
that we call root KMS.

00:10:57.650 --> 00:11:00.650
And root KMS is much smaller,
has maybe a dozen keys

00:11:00.650 --> 00:11:02.890
that are really critical
to Google's infrastructure,

00:11:02.890 --> 00:11:06.810
and runs in special security
machines in our data centers.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:10.070
Root KMS is, of course, itself
protected with something

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.950
called the root KMS master key.

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:17.000
So the root KMS master key is
held in distributed memory,

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:19.590
in a peer-to-peer infrastructure
that we'll call the root KMS

00:11:19.590 --> 00:11:21.170
master key distributor.

00:11:21.170 --> 00:11:23.720
That's the fun part about
taking internal code names

00:11:23.720 --> 00:11:25.344
and trying to explain
them externally--

00:11:25.344 --> 00:11:28.550
I end up with root KMS
master key distributor.

00:11:28.550 --> 00:11:31.250
So the root KMS master key
distributor is kept in memory,

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.600
and basically what happens is
a new job spins up and says,

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:36.600
I'm the root KMS master
key distributor, give me

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:38.194
the root KMS master key.

00:11:38.194 --> 00:11:39.860
And it has a list of
all the other hosts

00:11:39.860 --> 00:11:42.311
that are running the root
KMS master key distributor.

00:11:42.311 --> 00:11:44.810
Asks it for the master key, and
they pass it back and forth,

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:47.930
and you have another
instance up and running.

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:50.450
I think it's explained in much
better detail in our security

00:11:50.450 --> 00:11:51.530
design white paper.

00:11:51.530 --> 00:11:52.820
But the reason this
can work in practice

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.528
is that every job that
spins up at Google

00:11:54.528 --> 00:11:55.760
has a unique identity.

00:11:55.760 --> 00:11:58.814
And so when something spins
up and asks for the root KMS

00:11:58.814 --> 00:12:00.980
master key distributor, we
know that that's actually

00:12:00.980 --> 00:12:03.230
what it is-- it's not
faking and potentially

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:08.605
another rogue piece
of code or something.

00:12:08.605 --> 00:12:09.980
So what that means
in practice is

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:13.530
that your data is stored
locally to wherever it is,

00:12:13.530 --> 00:12:14.780
with the data encryption keys.

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:18.790
The key encryption keys are
stored on prod machines in KMS.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:22.250
Root KMS is run on security
machines in our data centers.

00:12:22.250 --> 00:12:27.807
And as long as at least one
instance of the root KMS master

00:12:27.807 --> 00:12:30.900
key distributor is running in
memory globally, in theory,

00:12:30.900 --> 00:12:33.130
we're fine, right?

00:12:33.130 --> 00:12:36.076
OK, now what if everything
at Google goes down?

00:12:36.076 --> 00:12:37.700
What if every single
Google data center

00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:39.230
loses power simultaneously?

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:41.636
We have nothing running
in memory anymore.

00:12:41.636 --> 00:12:43.160
I love working at Google.

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:45.622
People think about
problems like this.

00:12:45.622 --> 00:12:47.330
What that means in
practice is that we've

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:52.670
written that key to disk,
to security hardware kept

00:12:52.670 --> 00:12:57.440
in physical safes, in at least
two globally-distributed Google

00:12:57.440 --> 00:13:01.654
locations that less than
20 people have access to.

00:13:01.654 --> 00:13:04.070
And so if everything at Google
were to completely go down,

00:13:04.070 --> 00:13:07.419
someone would go open a safe,
get our root KMS master key,

00:13:07.419 --> 00:13:09.710
put it back into production
as we turn back up our data

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:10.615
centers.

00:13:10.615 --> 00:13:13.380
Your data will still be there,
and it will still be encrypted.

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:14.880
So that's how--
that's kind of cool.

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:17.300
So again, a quick
summary-- that's

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.700
many, many layers of
the key hierarchy there.

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.850
But we can do this in order
to encrypt your data at scale

00:13:22.850 --> 00:13:29.591
at Google, by default. The sexy
line that you get to say is,

00:13:29.591 --> 00:13:31.590
"Google Cloud Platform
encrypts customer content

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:33.020
stored at rest,
without any action

00:13:33.020 --> 00:13:35.353
required from the customer,
using one or more encryption

00:13:35.353 --> 00:13:36.510
mechanisms.

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:41.250
#GoogleNext17" So there's a
white paper on our website that

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:42.405
explains this in detail.

00:13:42.405 --> 00:13:43.780
All the same
diagrams and things.

00:13:43.780 --> 00:13:45.690
Everything I'm saying
right now is public.

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.260
And there are a few
exceptions around logs

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:49.110
and that type of thing,
but those are all

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:50.318
explained in the white paper.

00:13:50.318 --> 00:13:52.410
We're not hiding anything here.

00:13:52.410 --> 00:13:54.137
So that's what you
get by default.

00:13:54.137 --> 00:13:56.470
And once you explain this to
your CSO, and they're like,

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:58.060
no, no, no, I still need
to manage my own keys.

00:13:58.060 --> 00:13:59.200
You're like, great.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:01.840
Google has this other
thing, called Cloud KMS.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:04.735
Cloud KMS is now in
general availability.

00:14:04.735 --> 00:14:07.404
It lets you generate, use,
rotate, and destroy keys,

00:14:07.404 --> 00:14:10.070
and has a couple features that I
want to just highlight quickly.

00:14:10.070 --> 00:14:11.530
One is automatic rotation.

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:16.300
What that means in
practice is that we

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:20.410
can generate a new crypto
version for you, that you

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:22.650
can use to encrypt data
on an automatic basis.

00:14:22.650 --> 00:14:26.230
So you can say, for example,
as a rule, every 60 days I want

00:14:26.230 --> 00:14:28.360
a new crypto version created.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:30.840
And we don't re-encrypt
the old data, but we--

00:14:30.840 --> 00:14:32.950
you can therefore limit
the amount of data

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.480
encrypted with any single key.

00:14:34.480 --> 00:14:36.250
The second item here
is a 24-hour delay

00:14:36.250 --> 00:14:37.930
for key destruction.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:41.920
So I'm very terrified about a
customer potentially destroying

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.230
an encryption key without
realizing that it encrypts

00:14:44.230 --> 00:14:47.090
data, and therefore,
losing access to that data,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:50.050
for all intents and purposes.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:51.990
So we built in a
purposeful 24-hour delay,

00:14:51.990 --> 00:14:54.027
so that when you
say, destroy key,

00:14:54.027 --> 00:14:56.110
it goes into a kind of a
temporary suspended state

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.870
for 24-hours, and you
can restore that key--

00:14:58.870 --> 00:15:01.990
again, to prevent
error, insiders,

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:04.600
any kind of other risk
that could happen there.

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:08.980
IAM and logging were integrated
with cloud IAM, have predefined

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:10.650
roles to allow for
separation of duties,

00:15:10.650 --> 00:15:13.000
that I'll talk about in
a second, and logging.

00:15:13.000 --> 00:15:14.860
We're integrated with
cloud autologging.

00:15:14.860 --> 00:15:17.290
Cloud autologging has
two kinds of logs.

00:15:17.290 --> 00:15:19.850
Admin activity logs--
which are on by default--

00:15:19.850 --> 00:15:22.060
which are things like
change IAM permission,

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:24.490
change key rotation
policy, et cetera.

00:15:24.490 --> 00:15:27.300
And it has data access logs--
which are not on by default,

00:15:27.300 --> 00:15:29.300
but you can turn on--
that will say things like,

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:31.570
was this key used to encrypt?

00:15:31.570 --> 00:15:36.680
Lastly, KMS is available via
our gcloud CLI, client libraries

00:15:36.680 --> 00:15:39.310
in seven languages, including
Go, Java, Python, and then

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.816
through the cloud console UI.

00:15:41.816 --> 00:15:43.440
I do often get a
question, so I'll just

00:15:43.440 --> 00:15:45.070
point out really quickly--

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.600
just like our KMS
internally, keys in cloud KMS

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:48.784
are not exportable.

00:15:48.784 --> 00:15:50.200
So somebody will
be like, hey, how

00:15:50.200 --> 00:15:53.290
come I can't see my encryption
key on the cloud console?

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.010
That's by design.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.521
You can't see the
encryption key.

00:15:55.521 --> 00:15:57.895
The only way for you to interact
with that encryption key

00:15:57.895 --> 00:15:59.707
is to send data via an API.

00:15:59.707 --> 00:16:01.540
You'll get back encrypted
or decrypted data,

00:16:01.540 --> 00:16:05.990
depending on the request
that you were making.

00:16:05.990 --> 00:16:08.160
Let's talk about a couple
of concepts in Cloud KMS,

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:10.430
so that we're all
on the same page.

00:16:10.430 --> 00:16:14.420
Object hierarchy-- so Cloud KMS
has the concept of CryptoKeys.

00:16:14.420 --> 00:16:17.840
CryptoKeys are objects
that sit inside KeyRings.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.090
KeyRings are just
groups of CryptoKeys.

00:16:20.090 --> 00:16:23.330
CryptoKeys are like
a named object that

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:24.920
has a set of CryptoKeyVersions.

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:27.969
The CryptoKeyVersions are the
actual cryptographic material.

00:16:27.969 --> 00:16:29.760
So when you're talking
about a new Version,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:32.051
we're talking about a new
set of cryptographic material

00:16:32.051 --> 00:16:34.330
that we're going to use to
encrypt or decrypt data.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:35.720
The CryptoKey
itself has nothing,

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:38.910
and no particular material
[? is hid to it. ?]

00:16:38.910 --> 00:16:42.420
Key states-- CryptoKeyVersions
can be in multiple states.

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:44.790
They're listed here--
enabled, disabled, scheduled

00:16:44.790 --> 00:16:46.880
for rotation, destroyed.

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.950
Enabled CryptoKeyVersions can
be used to encrypt and decrypt

00:16:49.950 --> 00:16:50.742
data.

00:16:50.742 --> 00:16:53.200
Disabled Versions means that
they cannot be used to encrypt

00:16:53.200 --> 00:16:54.640
and decrypt data.

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:56.616
So far, so good.

00:16:56.616 --> 00:16:57.990
There's another
important concept

00:16:57.990 --> 00:17:00.875
to grok here, which is that
of a primary key version.

00:17:00.875 --> 00:17:02.500
A primary version is
the version that's

00:17:02.500 --> 00:17:03.855
used to encrypt your data.

00:17:03.855 --> 00:17:05.355
So now that we
understand that, when

00:17:05.355 --> 00:17:09.869
we were talking about
key rotation earlier,

00:17:09.869 --> 00:17:10.970
rotating a key--

00:17:10.970 --> 00:17:12.900
what we actually do is
generate a new version

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:14.317
and make that the
primary version.

00:17:14.317 --> 00:17:16.400
So we're not talking about
re-encrypting old data,

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:18.150
but we're limiting
anything else that you

00:17:18.150 --> 00:17:20.450
want to encrypt with that key.

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:22.859
Scheduled for destruction
and destroyed--

00:17:22.859 --> 00:17:25.560
what happens in practice, when
I was talking about destruction

00:17:25.560 --> 00:17:28.290
earlier, is that when you
say, destroy this key,

00:17:28.290 --> 00:17:30.750
we move it into the state
scheduled for destruction,

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:32.799
and then after 24 hours
it becomes destroyed.

00:17:32.799 --> 00:17:34.590
If it's still in
scheduled for destruction,

00:17:34.590 --> 00:17:37.410
you can restore it, and
get back to something

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:38.540
like enabled or disabled.

00:17:42.892 --> 00:17:44.350
In terms of key
rotation, I already

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:48.540
explained what I meant by having
a new CryptoKeyVersion created

00:17:48.540 --> 00:17:49.870
and making that primary.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.786
You can do that
automatically, so like I said,

00:17:51.786 --> 00:17:53.770
set a policy-- every
30 days, every two

00:17:53.770 --> 00:17:56.269
days, whenever you want-- and
create a new CryptoKeyVersion.

00:17:56.269 --> 00:17:58.510
And you can also do that
manually, via an API call,

00:17:58.510 --> 00:17:59.610
or on the UI.

00:18:06.246 --> 00:18:07.620
And lastly,
separation of duties.

00:18:07.620 --> 00:18:10.670
I mentioned that we have some
predefined roles for IAM.

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:12.410
What that means
in practice is you

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:14.451
might want to have an
organizational control that

00:18:14.451 --> 00:18:17.060
says, the people who use
the encryption keys aren't

00:18:17.060 --> 00:18:19.404
the same people who manage
the encryption keys.

00:18:19.404 --> 00:18:20.820
So we set that up
for you already.

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:21.570
We have two roles.

00:18:21.570 --> 00:18:23.780
You can give Alice the
cloudkms.admin role

00:18:23.780 --> 00:18:26.811
and Bob the cloudkms.cryptoK
eyEncrypterDecrypter role.

00:18:26.811 --> 00:18:28.310
And that means that
Alice can manage

00:18:28.310 --> 00:18:30.710
who has access to the keys,
and rotations, all of that,

00:18:30.710 --> 00:18:34.904
and Bob can set up something to
encrypt data using those keys.

00:18:34.904 --> 00:18:37.570
What happens in practice is that
Bob will probably be a service,

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.080
so you'll probably want to
give something like a service

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:40.479
account that role.

00:18:40.479 --> 00:18:42.770
We also have encrypt-only
roles and decrypt-only roles,

00:18:42.770 --> 00:18:44.603
if that's something
that you're looking for.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:49.200
What makes Cloud KMS special?

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:50.850
Things that your SRE
is going to love--

00:18:50.850 --> 00:18:53.430
scalability, global
availability, and latency.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:56.187
Scalability-- you can have as
many KeyVersions as you want.

00:18:56.187 --> 00:18:58.020
So I had a customer
[? who wanted me to-- ?]

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.853
they're like, can I
have 1,000 key versions?

00:18:59.853 --> 00:19:01.690
I'm like, of course--

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:02.860
do you want a million?

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:04.320
You want 10 million?

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.030
Just hit us.

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:08.550
The reasoning behind
this is that we shouldn't

00:19:08.550 --> 00:19:11.400
have to limit how you can
encrypt data at scale, just

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:13.606
because you can't have as
many versions as you want.

00:19:13.606 --> 00:19:15.480
You should have as many
versions as you want.

00:19:15.480 --> 00:19:17.280
This also enables a
use case which I'll

00:19:17.280 --> 00:19:19.620
call cryptographic isolation.

00:19:19.620 --> 00:19:23.220
You might have the
desire for your data,

00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:25.764
that each of your
customers' data

00:19:25.764 --> 00:19:27.180
is encrypted with
a different key.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:30.550
But every row in a
table that corresponds

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:33.510
to a particular customer is
encrypted with a separate key.

00:19:33.510 --> 00:19:34.812
That's possible with Cloud KMS.

00:19:34.812 --> 00:19:37.020
You can implement that,
there's nothing stopping you.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:39.846
So go forth and encrypt.

00:19:39.846 --> 00:19:41.595
The second one here,
global availability--

00:19:41.595 --> 00:19:43.386
Cloud KMS is a regional service.

00:19:43.386 --> 00:19:44.760
So what that means
in practice is

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:49.590
that you pick a region in
which to create your resources,

00:19:49.590 --> 00:19:53.910
CryptoKeys and KeyRings.

00:19:53.910 --> 00:19:57.930
CryptoKeys and KeyRings
can't be moved, or renamed,

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:00.460
or actually, in Cloud
KMSes case, deleted.

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:02.960
You can destroy all the crypto
materials associated to them,

00:20:02.960 --> 00:20:04.793
but you can't actually
delete that resource.

00:20:04.793 --> 00:20:08.846
And that's for logging,
auditing, historical purposes.

00:20:08.846 --> 00:20:10.470
So something like
global availability--

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:12.277
you can create a key
in, like, us-east-1,

00:20:12.277 --> 00:20:13.860
but you can still
access it in Europe.

00:20:13.860 --> 00:20:16.276
There's nothing stopping you
from using that key globally.

00:20:16.276 --> 00:20:18.930
So you don't have to have a
different key in each region.

00:20:18.930 --> 00:20:21.430
We have optimized the keys
in those regions for latency,

00:20:21.430 --> 00:20:26.950
but that's not preventing
you from using it elsewhere.

00:20:26.950 --> 00:20:28.990
And last one here-- latency.

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:31.470
Cloud KMS is very fast in
encrypt and decrypt operations,

00:20:31.470 --> 00:20:33.012
on the order of tens
of milliseconds.

00:20:33.012 --> 00:20:35.136
So you should be able to
use it in the serving path

00:20:35.136 --> 00:20:36.580
of your application.

00:20:36.580 --> 00:20:38.220
You shouldn't
non-encrypt something

00:20:38.220 --> 00:20:40.636
because you're worried about
your application starting up.

00:20:40.636 --> 00:20:42.272
So this should not
be a limit again.

00:20:42.272 --> 00:20:45.060
Again, all things your
SREs are going to love.

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:47.490
And part of that is also
with our general availability

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:48.310
announcement.

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:51.570
We posted an SLA yesterday
morning around availability,

00:20:51.570 --> 00:20:52.520
around uptime.

00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:53.978
So you can see that
on our website,

00:20:53.978 --> 00:20:57.840
and that comes as part
of standard Cloud KMS.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:00.070
OK, I'll take up some water.

00:21:07.750 --> 00:21:14.200
So we've been talking
about secret management--

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:17.330
sorry, encryption keys.

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:18.120
What did I do?

00:21:24.250 --> 00:21:26.470
Yes, please-- please help
me with my notes, please.

00:21:29.890 --> 00:21:32.610
We've been talking about
encryption at rest encryption.

00:21:32.610 --> 00:21:34.440
So one of the first
uses Cloud KMS

00:21:34.440 --> 00:21:36.820
might be encryption at
scale-- bulk data encryption.

00:21:36.820 --> 00:21:39.320
So you might want to implement
something like a multiple-key

00:21:39.320 --> 00:21:41.486
hierarchy-- like I just
described that Google does--

00:21:41.486 --> 00:21:42.370
to encrypt your data.

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:44.170
That lets you have
encryption at scale.

00:21:44.170 --> 00:21:46.339
A second use case is
around secret management.

00:21:46.339 --> 00:21:48.130
So you might have some
small pieces of data

00:21:48.130 --> 00:21:52.450
that you want to keep
secret in your application.

00:21:52.450 --> 00:21:54.610
One such example
would be something

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:58.540
like API keys, OAuth tokens,
some super-special credentials

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:00.039
that you have,
something like that.

00:22:00.039 --> 00:22:02.080
And typically, developers
that I've talked to now

00:22:02.080 --> 00:22:04.280
are keeping those in
a variety of places.

00:22:04.280 --> 00:22:07.720
But they include things
like directly in the code,

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:11.710
in the metadata of a VM,
in a deployment manager,

00:22:11.710 --> 00:22:14.290
in a configuration file, in
a bunch of different places.

00:22:14.290 --> 00:22:17.110
And that's all fine, and--

00:22:17.110 --> 00:22:19.360
I think we have multiple
slides happening.

00:22:19.360 --> 00:22:20.365
OK, cool.

00:22:20.365 --> 00:22:23.510
We'll figure it out.

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:24.410
Cool.

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:28.724
So you can store secrets in a
variety of places right now.

00:22:28.724 --> 00:22:30.640
What we're also going
to be publishing today--

00:22:30.640 --> 00:22:32.560
publishing yesterday--
on our website

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:36.050
is a set of questions for you to
think about secret management,

00:22:36.050 --> 00:22:38.290
and an option for how you
could implement that at GCP

00:22:38.290 --> 00:22:40.280
with what's currently available.

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:43.000
So what I mean by that is that
there's a variety of questions

00:22:43.000 --> 00:22:45.134
you might be having
around secret management.

00:22:45.134 --> 00:22:47.050
You probably want to
optimize for a few things

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:48.542
around security
and functionality.

00:22:48.542 --> 00:22:50.500
Strong security-- something
like authorization,

00:22:50.500 --> 00:22:52.666
like, who should actually
have access to the secret?

00:22:52.666 --> 00:22:54.580
How do I manage
access to that secret?

00:22:54.580 --> 00:22:56.200
Version management--
how do I know

00:22:56.200 --> 00:22:58.150
that all my applications
have the same version

00:22:58.150 --> 00:23:02.260
of the secret right now, and
that they're all in sync?

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:04.960
Encryption-- are my
secrets encrypted at rest?

00:23:04.960 --> 00:23:08.597
These types of questions.

00:23:08.597 --> 00:23:09.930
I'm not going to click anything.

00:23:15.619 --> 00:23:16.410
Give me one second.

00:23:18.820 --> 00:23:20.570
So what we're hosting
on our website today

00:23:20.570 --> 00:23:22.309
is not a be-all and end-all.

00:23:22.309 --> 00:23:24.350
You can think-- there's
probably a couple of ways

00:23:24.350 --> 00:23:26.935
you can improve on just
hard coding secrets in code.

00:23:26.935 --> 00:23:29.060
The first one would be just
to encrypt those things

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:32.630
that are hard coded in code,
so that they are more protected

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:34.760
than if any of your
developers could have access,

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:36.671
but some of your
developers have access.

00:23:36.671 --> 00:23:38.420
A secondary improvement
could be something

00:23:38.420 --> 00:23:40.820
like putting all of your
secrets in a bucket,

00:23:40.820 --> 00:23:43.100
and encrypted that bucket--

00:23:43.100 --> 00:23:44.600
like a Google Cloud
Storage Bucket--

00:23:44.600 --> 00:23:46.100
and putting the
right IAM, the right

00:23:46.100 --> 00:23:48.390
logging information,
all that, all around it.

00:23:48.390 --> 00:23:51.290
Another option might be to use
a third-party secret management

00:23:51.290 --> 00:23:52.760
solution And I
think, again, this

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:54.740
is another array of
options and complexity,

00:23:54.740 --> 00:23:56.300
like, what are you
looking for out of this?

00:23:56.300 --> 00:23:58.520
If you're just looking to
limit a little bit more access,

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:00.311
you're looking for
additional auditability,

00:24:00.311 --> 00:24:04.910
or you're looking for more smart
features, like secret rotation,

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:07.380
you might pick
something more complex.

00:24:07.380 --> 00:24:09.380
So what we published is
a bit more explanation

00:24:09.380 --> 00:24:13.490
as to how we do it, with Cloud
Storage Bucket, Cloud KMS, IAM,

00:24:13.490 --> 00:24:15.260
and cloud autologging.

00:24:15.260 --> 00:24:16.970
It's not the best solution.

00:24:16.970 --> 00:24:19.862
It's not the best solution for
your environment, necessarily.

00:24:19.862 --> 00:24:22.070
But it's one way you can
think about managing secrets

00:24:22.070 --> 00:24:24.680
in a central way, securely.

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:25.757
Awesome, thank you.

00:24:30.430 --> 00:24:32.940
The last option that I
talked about earlier--

00:24:32.940 --> 00:24:37.420
so we had default
encryption, we had Cloud KMS,

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:43.880
and we had customer-supplied
encryption keys.

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:45.969
And customer-supplied
encryption keys--

00:24:45.969 --> 00:24:48.260
so what happens here in
practice is that you provide us

00:24:48.260 --> 00:24:51.240
with a 256-bit string.

00:24:51.240 --> 00:24:53.660
We assume it's probably
an AES-256 key.

00:24:53.660 --> 00:24:56.240
We combine that with a
random cryptographic nonce--

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.430
so that the
randomness of that is

00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:00.230
sure to be at least
within level--

00:25:00.230 --> 00:25:03.872
and keep that in memory, and
use that to encrypt your data.

00:25:03.872 --> 00:25:05.830
So we talked earlier
about data encryption keys

00:25:05.830 --> 00:25:07.130
and key encryption keys.

00:25:07.130 --> 00:25:10.910
What this means in practice
is, for data objects in GCS,

00:25:10.910 --> 00:25:14.310
images, instances,
and VMs in GCE,

00:25:14.310 --> 00:25:19.230
we can use what you provided us
plus that cryptographic nonce

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:20.910
as the key encryption key.

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:23.100
The more complex thing
here is that you might not

00:25:23.100 --> 00:25:27.300
have a really easy way of
managing those keys locally,

00:25:27.300 --> 00:25:29.910
because we need you to pass
us that key as part of an API

00:25:29.910 --> 00:25:30.750
call.

00:25:30.750 --> 00:25:33.660
Since we don't store that
key on disk, only in memory,

00:25:33.660 --> 00:25:36.882
and only for the amount of time
required for that transaction,

00:25:36.882 --> 00:25:39.090
every time that you would
need to access that object,

00:25:39.090 --> 00:25:42.060
or write that object, or spin
up that instance, or whatever,

00:25:42.060 --> 00:25:44.890
you need to re-provide us
that key in that API call.

00:25:44.890 --> 00:25:46.770
So what we're announcing
today is a couple

00:25:46.770 --> 00:25:48.853
of partner integrations
to make that significantly

00:25:48.853 --> 00:25:50.930
easier for you.

00:25:50.930 --> 00:25:51.757
Yes.

00:25:51.757 --> 00:25:54.090
So if you're looking to use
customer-supplied encryption

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.070
keys, you basically need
to have a KMS on premise.

00:25:57.070 --> 00:25:58.861
We have a variety of
partners who are going

00:25:58.861 --> 00:26:02.330
to help us do this for you.

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:04.370
For customers without
existing KMS solutions,

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:06.590
you can look to work with them.

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:08.660
So using KeyNexus products--

00:26:08.660 --> 00:26:11.090
KeyNexus Enterprise On-Prem,
and KeyNexus Cloud--

00:26:11.090 --> 00:26:15.440
you can provision a key
both for GCE via gcloud,

00:26:15.440 --> 00:26:17.990
or for GCS via gsutil.

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:20.420
Using Thales nShield HSMs--

00:26:20.420 --> 00:26:23.340
which are FIPS 140-2 level
3 compliant-- so if that's

00:26:23.340 --> 00:26:26.330
a requirement that you have
around FIPS compliance,

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:29.039
you can supply a key
for use with GCE.

00:26:29.039 --> 00:26:31.580
Using Virtru, you can supply a
Virtru generated a key for use

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:33.380
with GCE.

00:26:33.380 --> 00:26:35.816
Using Ionic's products--

00:26:35.816 --> 00:26:37.190
Ionic Protect for
cloud storage--

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:39.530
you can supply a key
for use with GCS.

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:40.780
And using Gemalto's products--

00:26:40.780 --> 00:26:44.120
SafeNet KeySecure-- a
product, again, a key for GCS.

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:46.820
So KeyNexus right now is
supporting GCE and GCS;

00:26:46.820 --> 00:26:49.836
Thales and Virtru, GCE;
and Ionic and Gemalto, GCS.

00:26:49.836 --> 00:26:51.710
And we're going to expand
that in the future,

00:26:51.710 --> 00:26:53.680
so you have even
more options for how

00:26:53.680 --> 00:26:57.360
you can do key management
with our partners.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:27:00.360
We hope that you find these much
simpler than managing your keys

00:27:00.360 --> 00:27:02.600
on-prem, and again, love
to hear any feedback

00:27:02.600 --> 00:27:04.260
on that functionality.

00:27:04.260 --> 00:27:06.110
So again, how does
this work in practice

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:07.280
for all of these options?

00:27:07.280 --> 00:27:08.590
We have a storage object.

00:27:08.590 --> 00:27:10.298
That storage object
is split into chunks.

00:27:10.298 --> 00:27:12.650
The chunks are encrypted
with data encryption keys.

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:14.300
The data encryption keys are
encrypted with key encryption

00:27:14.300 --> 00:27:15.050
keys.

00:27:15.050 --> 00:27:17.090
And the key encryption key will
come from a variety of places,

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:18.740
depending on what you're
trying to do, right?

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:20.540
It'll come from default
Google encryption,

00:27:20.540 --> 00:27:23.630
using our internal KMS.

00:27:23.630 --> 00:27:26.510
Right now you can implement a
similar model using application

00:27:26.510 --> 00:27:27.965
layer encryption for Cloud KMS.

00:27:27.965 --> 00:27:29.840
Or you can use a
customer-supplied encryption

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:32.090
key, where you provide
us with some bits--

00:27:32.090 --> 00:27:34.740
either directly or via partner,
via one of our five partners--

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.337
and we'll add a
cryptographic nonce,

00:27:36.337 --> 00:27:37.670
and then use that in our system.

00:27:40.180 --> 00:27:43.000
So that's, I think, all I was
actually going to talk about.

